#import "statements.typ": *
#import "diagrams/38a-morphisms.typ": frobenius-morphisms

== Grothendieck Groups <sec:grothendieck-groups>
=== Grothendieck Groups and Frobenius Functors <ss:grothendieck-frobenius>

Let $R$ be a commutative ring, and let $G$ be a finite group. We recall from
§@i:ss:grothendieck-groups that the Grothendieck group $G_0 lr((R G))$ of the
group ring $R G$ is the additive group generated by symbols $[M]$ corresponding
to isomorphism classes $(M)$ of f.g. left $R G$-modules, with defining relations
$ [M] = [M'] + [M''], $
for each ses (short exact sequence)
$ 0 -> M' -> M -> M'' -> 0 $
of f.g. left $R G$-modules.
#source(19)

In case $R$ is a field $K$, we proved in §@i:ss:grothendieck-groups that
$G_0 lr((K G))$ has the structure of a commutative ring. The multiplication in
$G_0 lr((K G))$ was defined in such a way that
$ [M][N] = [M times.o_K N] $
for f.g. left $K G$-modules $M$ and $N$, where $M times.o_K N$ is the inner
tensor product (see Definition @i:def:outer-inner-tensor-products).

When $char K = 0$, we proved in @i:prop:grothendieck-character-ring that there
is an isomorphism of rings
$ G_0 lr((K G)) tilde.equiv op("ch") K G, $
where $op("ch") K G$ is the ring of virtual $K$-characters of $G$. The
isomorphism is given by $[M] -> mu$, for a left $K G$-module $M$, where $mu$ is
the $K$-character of $G$ afforded by $M$.

A similar result was established in §@i:sec:brauer-characters for
$G_0 lr((k G))$, where $k$ is a field of characteristic $p > 0$ associated with
a $p$-modular system in which $K$ is sufficiently large (see
§@i:sec:decomposition-map). As shown in Proposition
@i:prop:grothendieck-brauer-character-ring, we have an isomorphism of rings,
$ G_0 lr((k G)) tilde.equiv op("Bch") k G, $
which maps an element $t in G_0 lr((k G))$ onto the virtual Brauer character
associated with it.

In the cases described above, Grothendieck groups provide no more than a
reformulation of results about character rings. The same is true, at a deeper
level, for the induction theorems of §§@i:sec:artin-brauer-induction and
@i:sec:induction-arbitrary-fields. A typical induction theorem asserts that, for
a field $K$, we have
$ G_0 lr((K G)) = sum_(H in cal(E)_K) op("ind")_H^G lr((G_0 lr((K H)))), $
where $cal(E)_K$ is a family of subgroups of $G$ depending on $K$, and
$op("ind")_H^G$ is the induction map from $K H$-modules to $K G$-modules. These
theorems describe the _additive_ structure of Grothendieck groups
$G_0 lr((K G))$, but their proofs use the _multiplicative_ structure. In
particular, they depend on the fact that
$sum_(H in cal(E)_K) op("ind")_H^G lr((G_0 lr((K H))))$ is an ideal in the ring
$G_0 lr((K G))$; this follows from the identity in the ring $G_0 lr((K G))$,
proved in @i:lem:grothendieck-induction-product, that
$
  (op("ind")_H^G u) v = op("ind")_H^G lr((u dot op("res")_H^G v)),
  quad u in G_0 lr((K H)), quad v in G_0 lr((K G)).
$
In turn, this formula extends the identity
@i:lem:class-function-induction-product for complex-valued class functions:
$
  psi^G dot zeta = (psi dot zeta_H)^G,
  quad psi in cf_(CC) lr((H)), quad zeta in cf_(CC) lr((G)).
$

#source(20)
Algebraic $K$-theory begins with the study of Grothendieck groups
$G_0 lr((R G))$, for commutative rings $R$ which are not necessarily fields.
Among other things, we would like to have induction theorems like those in
§§@i:sec:artin-brauer-induction and @i:sec:induction-arbitrary-fields, which
depend on introducing multiplicative structures on Grothendieck groups.

In this subsection, we present an axiomatic approach to these ideas, due to T.
Y. Lam @bib:Lam1968a (see also Swan–Evans @bib:SwanEvans1970). Extensions and
variations of the results of this section, by Green @bib:Green1971 and Dress
@bib:Dress1973, will be taken up later, in Chapter @ch:representation-rings. We
start with Lam’s axiomatization of the functor $G -> G_0 lr((R G))$, and its
relation to induction and restriction, in cases where $G_0 lr((R G))$ is a
commutative ring.

#definition[
  A _Frobenius functor_ $F$ consists of the data:
  #case-list(
    numbering: "(i)",
    [
      #case[
        There is a correspondence $G -> F(G)$, which assigns to each finite
        group $G$ a commutative ring $F(G)$
      ] <case:frobenius-functor-commutative-ring>
    ],
    [
      #case[
        _(Restriction)_ For each inclusion $i: H <= G$ of groups, there exists a
        homomorphism of commutative rings
        $ i^*: F(G) -> F(H). $
        These ring homomorphisms satisfy the condition that
        $ (j i)^* = i^* j^* $
        for each composition of inclusions $H <=_i K <=_j G$. Further, if $H =
        G$, and $i: H <= G$ is the identity map, then $i^*$ is also the identity
        map.
      ] <case:frobenius-functor-restriction>
    ],
    [
      #case[
        _(Induction)_ For each inclusion $i: H <= G$, there exists a
        homomorphism of additive groups
        $ i_*: F(H) -> F(G) $
        such that $i_* = op("id")$ if $H = G$, and
        $ (j i)_* = j_* i_* $
        for each composite inclusion as in (ii).
      ] <case:frobenius-functor-induction>
    ],
    [
      #case[
        _(Frobenius identity)_ For each inclusion $i: H <= G$, we have the
        identity in the ring $F(G)$:
        $
          x dot i_* y = i_* lr(((i^* x) dot y)), quad x in F(G), quad y in F(H).
        $
      ] <case:frobenius-functor-frobenius-identity>
    ],
  )
] <def:frobenius-functor>
#idx("Frobenius functor", target: [@def:frobenius-functor])
#idx("Frobenius identity", target: [@def:frobenius-functor])
#idx("Restriction map", target: [@def:frobenius-functor])

#remark[
  It is useful to understand Definition @def:frobenius-functor in terms of
  category theory (see §@i:ss:categories-functors). Let $cal(G)$ be the category
  of all finite groups, with morphisms given by inclusions (that is,
  monomorphisms). When the conditions of @def:frobenius-functor hold, we have
  two functors defined on $cal(G)$:
  #source(21)
  #enum(
    numbering: "(i)",
    [The first is a contravariant functor from $cal(G)$ into the category of
      commutative rings $cal(R)$ (with ring homomorphisms as morphisms). This
      functor, called _restriction_, maps each object $G in cal(G)$ onto an
      object $F(G) in cal(R)$, and carries each inclusion $i: H <= G$ onto a
      homomorphism of rings $i^*: F(G) -> F(H)$.],
    [The second is a covariant functor from $cal(G)$ into $cal(A) cal(B)$, the
      category of abelian groups. This functor, called _induction_, maps each
      $G in cal(G)$ onto $F(G) in cal(A) cal(B)$, and carries each inclusion
      $i: H <= G$ onto a homomorphism of additive groups $i_*: F(H) -> F(G)$.],
  )
  The superscript on $i^*$ indicates that the functor involved (in this case,
  restriction) is contravariant. The subscript on $i_*$ means that the induction
  functor is covariant.
] <rem:frobenius-categorical-description>

By the remarks preceding @def:frobenius-functor, we have as our first example:

#proposition[
  Let $K$ be an arbitrary field. Then $G_0 lr((K G))$ is a commutative ring for
  each finite group $G$. The operations $op("res")_H^G$ and $op("ind")_H^G$ on
  $K H$-modules and $K G$-modules extend to maps
  $i^*: G_0 lr((K G)) -> G_0 lr((K H))$ and
  $i_*: G_0 lr((K H)) -> G_0 lr((K G))$ whenever $H <= G$. The data
  $ {F(G) = G_0 lr((K G)), i^*, i_*} $
  define a Frobenius functor.
] <prop:field-grothendieck-frobenius>

We omit the proof, which is straightforward, using the results mentioned
earlier. In particular, the extensions of the operations $op("res")_H^G$ and
$op("ind")_H^G$ to Grothendieck groups, and the Frobenius identity, have already
been explained in §@i:ss:induction-positive-characteristic.

The extension of Proposition @prop:field-grothendieck-frobenius to
$G_0 lr((R G))$, for a commutative ring $R$, is not immediate; indeed, as we
shall see, $G_0 lr((R G))$ is not necessarily a commutative ring. To avoid this
difficulty, we introduce the concept of an $R G$-lattice, that is a left
$R G$-module which is $R$-projective and f.g./$R$ (see
§@i:ss:contragredient-modules).

#definition[
  Let $R$ be a commutative ring, and $G$ a finite group. We define
  $G_0^R lr((R G))$ to be the Grothendieck group associated (as in
  §@i:ss:grothendieck-groups) with the category of left $R G$-lattices.
] <def:lattice-grothendieck-group>
#nidx(
  "$G_0 lr((A)), G_0^R lr((Lambda)), G_0^t lr((Lambda))$",
  "Grothendieck groups",
  target: [@def:lattice-grothendieck-group],
)
#idx("Grothendieck group", target: [@def:lattice-grothendieck-group])
#idx("Lattice", target: [@def:lattice-grothendieck-group])

#proposition[
  The Grothendieck group $G_0^R lr((R G))$, for a finite group $G$ and a
  commutative ring $R$, is a commutative ring. Multiplication is given by
  $ [M][N] = [M times.o_R N] $
  for $R G$-lattices $M$ and $N$, where $M times.o_R N$ is the inner tensor
  product.
] <prop:lattice-grothendieck-ring>
#idx("Grothendieck ring", target: [@prop:lattice-grothendieck-ring])
#proof[
  The argument is essentially the same as that given in
  §@i:ss:grothendieck-groups, showing that $G_0 lr((K G))$ is a commutative ring
  when $K$ is a field. Let us point out a few main steps which must be
  considered in the more general situation.
  #source(22)
  First of all, if $M$ and $N$ are $R G$-lattices, then so is their inner tensor
  product $M times.o_R N$. It is clear that this tensor product is an f.g. left
  $R G$-module. But it is also an $R$-lattice, since if $M$ and $N$ are
  $R$-lattices, then so is $M times.o_R N$. For we may choose $R$-modules $M'$
  and $N'$ such that $M plus.o M'$ and $N plus.o N'$ are $R$-free, and then use
  @i:eq:tensor-direct-sums and the fact that $R times.o_R R tilde.equiv R$ (see
  @i:eq:tensor-regular-module).

  Second, given any ses
  $ 0 -> N' -> N -> N'' -> 0 $ <eq:lattice-short-exact-sequence>
  of $R G$-lattices, and an arbitrary $R G$-lattice $M$, the sequence
  $ 0 -> M times.o_R N' -> M times.o_R N -> M times.o_R N'' -> 0 $
  <eq:lattice-tensor-exact-sequence>
  is also an exact sequence of $R G$-lattices. This follows, since $M$ is
  $R$-projective and hence $R$-flat, by @i:prop:projective-is-flat.

  We then let $upright(bold(F))$ be the free abelian group with a basis ${(M)}$
  consisting of isomorphism classes $(M)$ of $R G$-lattices. The operation of
  inner tensor product clearly gives $upright(bold(F))$ the structure of a
  commutative ring. The kernel of the natural map from $upright(bold(F))$ to
  $G_0^R lr((R G))$, given by $(M) -> [M]$, is generated by all elements
  $(N) - (N') - (N'')$ arising from ses’s @eq:lattice-short-exact-sequence as
  above. The exactness of @eq:lattice-tensor-exact-sequence shows that the
  kernel is an ideal in the commutative ring $upright(bold(F))$, and hence
  $G_0^R lr((R G))$ is also a commutative ring, with the required
  multiplication.
]

The above argument fails to show that $G_0 lr((R G))$ is a commutative ring, for
an arbitrary commutative ring $R$, since the sequence
@eq:lattice-tensor-exact-sequence may not be exact if $M$ is not $R$-projective.
The proof given above does show, however, that for each ses
@eq:lattice-short-exact-sequence of f.g. $R G$-_modules_, and an arbitrary
$R G$-lattice $M$, the sequence @eq:lattice-tensor-exact-sequence is exact. Thus
multiplication by $[M]$, for an $R G$-lattice $M$, is a well-defined operation
in $G_0 lr((R G))$. Therefore, $G_0 lr((R G))$ becomes a module over the
commutative ring $G_0^R lr((R G))$, with
$
  [M] dot [N] = [M times.o_R N]
$
for an $R G$-lattice $M$ and a f.g. $R G$-module $N$. This leads us to the
following:

#definition[
  A _Frobenius module_ $M$ over a Frobenius functor $F$ consists of the data:
  #enum(
    numbering: "(i)",
    [There is a correspondence which assigns to each finite group $G$ an
      $F(G)$-module $M(G)$.],
    [For each inclusion $i: H <= G$, there exist a pair of homomorphisms of
      additive groups
      $ i^*: M(G) -> M(H) quad ("restriction") $
      and
      $ i_*: M(H) -> M(G) quad ("induction") $
      such that $i^* = i_* =$ identity map, in case $H = G$, and satisfying the
      same transitivity laws as in @def:frobenius-functor for composite
      inclusions.],
    [For each inclusion $i: H <= G$, we have
      $
        i^* lr((x m)) = i^* lr((x)) i^* lr((m)), quad x in F(G), quad m in M(G),
      $
      as well as the Frobenius identities,
      $
        x dot i_* lr((m')) = i_* lr(((i^* x) dot m')), quad x in F(G), quad
        m' in M(H),
      $
      and
      $
        (i_* x') dot m = i_* lr((x' dot i^* m)), quad x' in F(H), quad m in
        M(G).
      $],
  )
] <def:frobenius-module>
#idx("Frobenius module", target: [@def:frobenius-module])
#source(23)

We then have:

#proposition[
  Let $R$ be an arbitrary commutative ring. The correspondence
  $G -> G_0^R lr((R G))$, for each finite group $G$, together with the usual
  restriction and induction maps, is a Frobenius functor. The correspondence
  $G -> G_0 lr((R G))$, together with restriction and induction, and the
  operation $G_0^R lr((R G)) times G_0 lr((R G)) -> G_0 lr((R G))$ given by
  $ [M] dot [N] = [M times.o_R N] $
  for each $R G$-lattice $M$ and each f.g. $R G$-module $N$, is a Frobenius
  module over the Frobenius functor $G -> G_0^R lr((R G))$.
] <prop:grothendieck-frobenius-module>

The proof is routine, after what has been said, except for the Frobenius
identities. These follow from @i:cor:induction-tensor-identity, which asserts
that for each $R G$-module $N$ and each $R H$-module $M$, there is an
isomorphism of $R G$-modules:
$ M^G times.o_R N tilde.equiv (M times.o_R N_H)^G, $
where $times.o_R$ denotes inner tensor products of $R G$-modules and
$R H$-modules, respectively. The rest of the proof of
@prop:grothendieck-frobenius-module is left as an exercise for the reader.

#definition[
  Let $F_1$ and $F_2$ be Frobenius functors. A _morphism of Frobenius functors_
  $phi: F_1 -> F_2$ consists of a family of homomorphisms of commutative rings
  $ phi_G: F_1 lr((G)) -> F_2 lr((G)), $
  one for each finite group $G$, which commute with restriction and induction.
  This means that for each inclusion $i: H <= G$, the following diagrams
  commute:
  #frobenius-morphisms

  #source(24)
  Similarly, for Frobenius modules $M_1$ and $M_2$ over a Frobenius functor $F$,
  a _morphism of Frobenius modules_ $mu: M_1 -> M_2$ consists of a family of
  homomorphisms of $F(G)$-modules
  $ mu_G: M_1 lr((G)) -> M_2 lr((G)), $
  one for each finite group $G$, such that $mu$ commutes with $i_*$ and $i^*$ in
  an analogous manner.
] <def:frobenius-morphism>
#idx("Frobenius functor", "morphism", target: [@def:frobenius-morphism])
#idx("Frobenius module", "morphism", target: [@def:frobenius-morphism])
#idx("Morphism", "of Frobenius functors", target: [@def:frobenius-morphism])

#examples(name: "Examples of Frobenius Modules and Morphisms")[
  Let $R$ denote an arbitrary commutative ring. By
  @prop:grothendieck-frobenius-module, the correspondence $G -> G_0^R lr((R G))$
  is a Frobenius functor, which we shall call $G_0^R$. Similarly, the
  correspondence $G -> G_0 lr((R G))$ is a Frobenius module over $G_0^R$, which
  we shall call $G_0$. We now give some other examples.
  #enum(
    numbering: "(i)",
    [The Frobenius functor $G_0^R$ is a Frobenius module over itself, in an
      obvious way.],
    [We have already pointed out that $G_0$ is a Frobenius module over the
      Frobenius functor $G_0^R$. Another example of a Frobenius module over the
      Frobenius functor $G_0^R$ is given by $M: G -> K_0 lr((R G))$, where
      $K_0 lr((R G))$ is the Grothendieck group of the category $cal(P)(R G)$
      consisting of all f.g. projective $R G$-modules (see
      §@i:ss:grothendieck-groups). There are three main points to verify:

      #enum(
        numbering: "(a)",
        [For each $R G$-lattice $L$ and each $P in cal(P)(R G)$, the inner
          tensor product $L times.o_R P$ also lies in $cal(P)(R G)$. We prove
          this, writing $times.o$ in place of $times.o_R$ for brevity. Since $P$
          is a direct summand of $(R G)^((k))$ for some $k$, $L times.o P$ is a
          direct summand of $L times.o (R G)^((k))$. The latter is
          $R G$-projective by Exercise @i:exc:regular-inner-tensor-projective,
          and thus $L times.o P in cal(P)(R G)$, as claimed.],
        [The proposed multiplication preserves relations in $G_0^R lr((R G))$,
          that is, for each ses of $R G$-lattices
          $ 0 -> L' -> L -> L'' -> 0, $
          and each $P in cal(P)(R G)$, we have
          $
            [L times.o P] = [L' times.o P] + [L'' times.o P] quad "in" K_0
            lr((R G)).
          $
          But this is clear since
          $ 0 -> L' times.o P -> L times.o P -> L'' times.o P -> 0 $
          is $R G$-exact, and splits over $R G$ because
          $L'' times.o P in cal(P)(R G)$.],
        [Relations in $K_0 lr((R G))$ are preserved, that is,
          $
            [L times.o (P plus.o P')] = [L times.o P] + [L times.o P'] quad
            "in" K_0 lr((R G))
          $
          for each $R G$-lattice $L$, and for $P, P' in cal(P)(R G)$. This is
          obvious from the isomorphism
          $
            L times.o (P plus.o P') tilde.equiv (L times.o P) plus.o (L
              times.o P').
          $],
      )
      #source(25)
      Now let $i^*$ and $i_*$ be given by restriction and induction,
      respectively. It follows that $M$ is a Frobenius module over $G_0^R$.],
    [The _Cartan map_ $c: K_0 lr((R G)) -> G_0 lr((R G))$ is the homomorphism of
      additive groups, which assigns to $[M] in K_0 lr((R G))$, for
      $M in cal(P)(R G)$, the corresponding element $[M]$ in $G_0 lr((R G))$
      (see Definition @i:def:cartan-homomorphism-matrix). It is easily verified
      that the Cartan map is a morphism of Frobenius modules over the Frobenius
      functor $G_0^R$.],
    [Let $mu: M_1 -> M_2$ be a morphism of Frobenius modules over the Frobenius
      functor $F$. Then $ker mu$ and $op("coker") mu$, defined in the obvious
      way, are also Frobenius modules over $F$.],
    [Let $f: R -> S$ be a homomorphism of commutative rings. We shall obtain
      morphisms of Frobenius modules from the operations of tensoring and
      restriction, using the homomorphism $f$. For example, $S times.o_R M$ is
      an $S G$-lattice, for an $R G$-lattice $M$, where $S$ is an $R$-module
      through the homomorphism $f: R -> S$. Moreover, $f$ defines a homomorphism
      of rings $R G -> S G$ for each finite group $G$, so that by restriction of
      scalars (§@i:ss:induction-frobenius), each $S G$-module can be viewed as
      an $R G$-module. In general, however, the map $[M] -> [S times.o_R M]$ is
      not a morphism of Frobenius modules over the Frobenius functor $G_0^R$,
      because tensoring with $S$ need not preserve exactness of sequences of
      $R G$-modules, without some additional hypothesis, such as the assumption
      that $S$ is flat over $R$. We shall now summarize several cases where
      tensoring and restriction define morphisms of Frobenius modules.],
  )
] <exm:frobenius-modules-morphisms>
#idx("Cartan map", target: [@exm:frobenius-modules-morphisms])

#proposition[
  Let $f: R -> S$ be a homomorphism of commutative rings, and let $G_0^R$ denote
  the Frobenius functor $G -> G_0^R lr((R G))$. Then
  #enum(
    numbering: "(i)",
    [The map $M -> S times.o_R M$, from $R G$-modules to $S G$-modules, defines
      a morphism of Frobenius modules over the Frobenius functor $G_0^R$, in the
      following cases:
      $
        G_0^R lr((R G)) & -> G_0^S lr((S G)); \
        G_0^R lr((R G)) & -> G_0 lr((S G)) quad "if" S "is a flat" R
                          "-module;" \
          K_0 lr((R G)) & -> K_0 lr((S G)).
      $
      In particular, the right-hand side is a Frobenius module over $G_0^R$ in
      each case.],
    [The map given by restriction of scalars, from $S G$-modules to
      $R G$-modules, using the homomorphism $f: R G -> S G$, defines a morphism
      of Frobenius modules over the Frobenius functor $G_0^R$, in the following
      cases:
      $
        G_0^S lr((S G)) & -> G_0^R lr((R G)) quad "if" S "is an" R "-lattice;" \
          G_0 lr((S G)) & -> G_0 lr((R G)) quad "if" S "is f.g./" R; \
          K_0 lr((S G)) & -> K_0 lr((R G)) quad "if" S "is an" R "-lattice."
      $],
  )
] <prop:frobenius-change-scalars>
#source(26)
The proof will be left to the reader.

As mentioned earlier, one reason for introducing Frobenius functors and
Frobenius modules is to prove induction theorems in more general situations than
those arising from character rings. To put it another way, we wish to compute
Frobenius functors $F(G)$ (or Frobenius modules $M(G)$) in terms of information
about $F(H)$, for $H$ some manageable type of subgroup (cyclic, elementary,
etc.) of $G$.

#definition[
  For each finite group $G$, let $cal(C)(G)$ denote a family of subgroups of
  $G$. Let $F$ be a Frobenius functor, and $M$ a Frobenius module over $F$. For
  each subgroup $H <= G$, let $i_H^*: F(G) -> F(H)$ (or $M(G) -> M(H)$) denote
  the restriction map, and let $(i_H)_*$ denote the induction map. Define
  $
    F(G)_(cal(C)) = sum_(H in cal(C)(G)) (i_H)_* F(H),
    quad M(G)_(cal(C)) = sum_(H in cal(C)(G)) (i_H)_* M(H).
  $
  Also define the dual objects
  $
    F(G)^(cal(C)) = inter.big_(H in cal(C)(G)) ker i_H^*,
    quad M(G)^(cal(C)) = inter.big_(H in cal(C)(G)) ker i_H^*.
  $
] <def:frobenius-family-subgroups>

#proposition[
  With the notation as above, we have for each finite group $G$:
  #case-list(
    numbering: "(i)",
    [
      #case[
        $F(G)_(cal(C))$ and $F(G)^(cal(C))$ are ideals of $F(G)$.
      ] <case:frobenius-family-ring-ideals>
    ],
    [
      #case[
        $M(G)_(cal(C))$ and $M(G)^(cal(C))$ are $F(G)$-submodules of $M(G)$.
      ] <case:frobenius-family-module-submodules>
    ],
    [
      #case[
        $F(G)_(cal(C)) dot M(G)^(cal(C)) = 0$,
        $F(G)^(cal(C)) dot M(G)_(cal(C)) = 0$,
        $F(G)_(cal(C)) dot F(G)^(cal(C)) = 0$.
      ] <case:frobenius-family-annihilation>
    ],
    [
      #case[
        $M(G) slash M(G)_(cal(C))$ and $M(G)^(cal(C))$ are modules over the ring
        $F(G) slash F(G)_(cal(C))$.
      ] <case:frobenius-family-quotient-modules>
    ],
    [
      #case[
        For each $i: H <= G$ such that $cal(C)(H) subset.eq cal(C)(G)$, we have
        $(i_H)_* lr((M(H)_(cal(C)))) subset.eq M(G)_(cal(C))$.
      ] <case:frobenius-family-subgroup-compatibility>
    ],
  )
] <prop:frobenius-family-ideals>
#proof[
  We first prove (ii), and obtain (i) by taking $M(G)$ equal to $F(G)$ in (ii).
  Clearly $M(G)_(cal(C))$ and $M(G)^(cal(C))$ are additive subgroups of $M(G)$.
  Now let $x in F(G)$, $m in M(H)$ and $n in M(G)^(cal(C))$, for some subgroup
  inclusion $i: H <= G$, where $H in cal(C)(G)$. Then by
  @def:frobenius-module(iii) we have
  $
    x dot i_* m = i_* lr((i_H^* x dot m)) in M(G)_(cal(C)),
    quad i_H^* lr((x n)) = i_H^* x dot i_H^* n = 0,
  $
  so (ii) follows, and hence (i), by the first remark.

  #source(27)
  Next, for $i: H <= G$, $H in cal(C)(G)$, $y in F(H)$, $n in M(G)^(cal(C))$, we
  have by another part of @def:frobenius-module(iii),
  $ i_* y dot n = i_* lr((y dot i_H^* n)) = 0, $
  proving the first statement of (iii). For the second, let
  $x in F(G)^(cal(C))$, $m in M(H)$; then
  $ x dot i_* m = i_* lr((i_H^* x dot m)) = 0. $
  The third follows from the first by taking $M = F$.

  Statement (iv) follows easily from (iii), together with the formula
  $ F(G)_(cal(C)) dot M(G) subset.eq M(G)_(cal(C)), $
  which also follows from the Frobenius identities.

  The last statement follows from the transitivity of $i_*$ over composite
  inclusions, and the proof is completed.
]

#corollary[
  #enum(
    numbering: "(i)",
    [If $F(G)_(cal(C)) = F(G)$, then $M(G)^(cal(C)) = 0$.],
    [If an integer $n$ annihilates $F(G) slash F(G)_(cal(C))$, it also
      annihilates $M(G) slash M(G)_(cal(C))$ and $M(G)^(cal(C))$.],
  )
] <cor:frobenius-induction-annihilators>

The proof of (ii) follows from @prop:frobenius-family-ideals(iv) and the
observation that if $n S = 0$ for a ring $S$, then $n dot M = 0$ for any module
$M$ over $S$. The first result follows from the second by taking $n = 1$.

We conclude this subsection with two applications of the preceding ideas. The
first is an extension of the Witt–Berman Theorem at characteristic $p$ (see
§@i:ss:induction-positive-characteristic), and the second is another proof that
the cokernel of the Cartan map is annihilated by a power of $p$.

#proposition[
  Let $(K, R, k)$ be a $p$-modular system, where $K$ and $k$ are fields with
  $char K = 0$, $char k = p > 0$. For each finite group $G$, let
  $cal(E)_K lr((G))$ be the family of $K$-elementary subgroups of $G$. Then
  #enum(
    numbering: "(i)",
    [$K_0 lr((k G))_(cal(E)_K) = K_0 lr((k G))$.],
    [Let $x in K_0 lr((k G))$. If $i_H^* x = 0$ for each subgroup inclusion
      $i_H: H <= G$, with $H in cal(E)_K$, then $x = 0$.],
    [Let $c: K_0 lr((k G)) -> G_0 lr((k G))$ be the Cartan map. Then
      $op("cok") c$ is annihilated by a power of $p$.],
  )
] <prop:modular-projective-induction>
#proof[
  In the terminology of Definition @def:frobenius-family-subgroups, Theorem
  @i:th:witt-berman-modular-grothendieck-induction gives
  $ G_0 lr((k G)) = G_0 lr((k G))_(cal(E)_K). $
  #source(28)
  Then the integer 1 annihilates $G_0 lr((k G)) slash G_0 lr((k G))_(cal(E)_K)$,
  and hence also annihilates $K_0 lr((k G)) slash K_0 lr((k G))_(cal(E)_K)$ by
  @cor:frobenius-induction-annihilators(ii), since $K_0$ is a Frobenius module
  over the Frobenius functor $G_0$ by @exm:frobenius-modules-morphisms(ii). (In
  this case we have $G_0 lr((k G)) = G_0^k lr((k G))$ for each finite group $G$,
  because $k$ is a field.) The second statement follows from Corollary
  @cor:frobenius-induction-annihilators(i), and the above remarks.

  For the third statement, we first assume that $K$ is sufficiently large. By
  @exm:frobenius-modules-morphisms(iii), the Cartan map
  $c: K_0 lr((k G)) -> G_0 lr((k G))$ is a morphism of Frobenius modules over
  the Frobenius functor $G -> G_0 lr((k G))$. By
  @exm:frobenius-modules-morphisms(iv), $op("cok") c$ is also a Frobenius module
  over $G_0 lr((k G))$. Since $K$ is sufficiently large, the family $cal(E)_K$
  of $K$-elementary subgroups of $G$ coincides with the family $cal(E)$ of
  elementary subgroups of $G$, and Theorem
  @i:th:witt-berman-modular-grothendieck-induction becomes
  $ G_0 lr((k G)) = G_0 lr((k G))_(cal(E)). $
  By the same argument used to prove part (i), it follows that
  $ op("cok") c = (op("cok") c)_(cal(E)). $
  By Exercise @i:exc:elementary-group-cartan-image, we have
  $|G|_p dot (op("cok") c)_(cal(E)) = 0$, and hence $|G|_p dot op("cok") c = 0$.
  The extension to an arbitrary field $K$ is then carried out by the same
  argument used in the proof of @i:th:cartan-map-cokernel-p-primary.
]

The preceding result extends an induction theorem from $G_0 lr((k G))$ to
$K_0 lr((k G))$, and really does no more than reinterpret an earlier argument
concerning the cokernel of the Cartan map. (The results can, of course, be
obtained without the machinery of Frobenius functors.) Our final result, due to
Swan @bib:Swan1960, will be proved later. Here, we shall use it to derive
several new induction theorems for $G_0^R lr((R G))$, for an arbitrary
commutative ring $R$.

#theorem(title: [Swan])[
  Let $R$ be a Dedekind ring with quotient field $K$, and let
  $overline(R) = R slash P$, for a maximal ideal $P$ in $R$. Let $cal(C)(G)$ be
  a family of subgroups of $G$, for each finite group $G$. Let $n in ZZ$ be such
  that
  $ n [G_0 lr((K G)) slash G_0 lr((K G))_(cal(C))] = 0. $
  Then also
  $
    n [G_0 lr((overline(R) G)) slash G_0 lr((overline(R) G))_(cal(C))] = 0
    quad "and" quad
    n^2 [G_0 lr((R G)) slash G_0 lr((R G))_(cal(C))] = 0.
  $
] <th:swan-grothendieck-annihilation>

A proof will be given in @lem:swan-induction-annihilators.

We next apply @th:swan-grothendieck-annihilation to obtain extensions of the
Artin Induction Theorem @i:th:artin-induction-explicit-coefficients and the
Witt–Berman Theorem @i:th:witt-berman-induction.

#corollary[
  For each finite group $G$, let $cal(C)(G)$ be the family of cyclic subgroups
  of $G$. Then
  #source(29)
  #enum(
    numbering: "(i)",
    [$|G|^2 [G_0^R lr((R G)) slash G_0^R lr((R G))_(cal(C))] = 0$,

      for an arbitrary commutative ring $R$, and],
    [$|G| [G_0^R lr((R G)) slash G_0^R lr((R G))_(cal(C))] = 0$,

      for any commutative ring $R$ of nonzero prime characteristic.],
  )
] <cor:lattice-cyclic-induction>
#proof[
  By Artin’s Induction Theorem @i:th:artin-induction-explicit-coefficients, and
  @th:swan-grothendieck-annihilation, it follows that $|G|^2$ annihilates
  $G_0^ZZ lr((ZZ G)) slash G_0^ZZ lr((ZZ G))_(cal(C))$. For an arbitrary
  commutative ring $R$, there is a homomorphism of rings $ZZ -> R$, making
  $G_0^R$ into a Frobenius module over $G_0^ZZ$, by
  @prop:frobenius-change-scalars(i). The first statement follows by Corollary
  @cor:frobenius-induction-annihilators. Further, if
  $overline(ZZ) = ZZ slash p ZZ$, then $|G|$ annihilates
  $G_0 lr((overline(ZZ) G)) slash G_0 lr((overline(ZZ) G))_(cal(C))$, for each
  finite group $G$, by @th:swan-grothendieck-annihilation. Then (ii) is
  immediate, since there exists a homomorphism of commutative rings
  $overline(ZZ) -> R$ for any commutative ring $R$ of characteristic $p$.
]

#corollary[
  Let $cal(H)(G)$ denote the family of hyper-elementary subgroups#footnote[
    See Volume I, @i:term:hyper-elementary-groups.
  ] of $G$. Then
  $
    G_0^R lr((R G))_(cal(H)) = G_0^R lr((R G)), quad G_0^R lr((R G))^(cal(H))
    = 0,
  $
  for every commutative ring $R$.
] <cor:lattice-hyperelementary-induction>

The proof is similar to the proof of @cor:lattice-cyclic-induction. By the
Solomon Induction Theorem @i:th:solomon-permutation-induction, or by the
Witt–Berman Theorem @i:th:witt-berman-induction, we have
$ G_0 lr((QQ G))_(cal(H)) = G_0 lr((QQ G)), $
for every finite group $G$. By @th:swan-grothendieck-annihilation, we have
$ G_0^ZZ lr((ZZ G))_(cal(H)) = G_0^ZZ lr((ZZ G)) $
for each finite group $G$, and the result follows, using the existence of a
homomorphism $ZZ -> R$, as in @cor:lattice-cyclic-induction.

#corollary[
  For each finite group $G$ and commutative ring $R$, we have
  $ |G|^2 [K_0 lr((R G)) slash K_0 lr((R G))_(cal(C))] = 0, $
  where $cal(C)(G)$ is the family of cyclic subgroups of $G$. If $R$ has nonzero
  prime characteristic, then for each $G$ we have
  $ |G| [K_0 lr((R G)) slash K_0 lr((R G))_(cal(C))] = 0. $
] <cor:projective-cyclic-induction>

The proof, which is an extension of the proof of @cor:lattice-cyclic-induction,
is left to the reader.
